00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:24.280
&gt;&gt; MADELEINE ALBRIGHT: Good morning, everybody.

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:25.280
Thank you very much for being here, and I
can only assure you that we're going to provide

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:26.280
you with a fascinating discussion.

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:27.280
I also want to welcome the Council on Foreign
Relations members from around the nation and

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:28.280
the world who are participating in this meeting
via teleconference.

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:29.280
On a housekeeping note, please completely
turn off -- not just vibrate, as I tend to

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:35.749
do myself with my cell phone -- to avoid any
interference with the sound system.

00:00:35.749 --> 00:00:41.010
And then also please, as a reminder, just
to tell you this meeting is on the record.

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:47.909
Now with all that out of the way, let me say
that how really, really pleased I am to introduce

00:00:47.909 --> 00:00:50.239
this woman foreign minister.

00:00:50.239 --> 00:00:55.010
I just have to tell you, when I arrived in
New York as our ambassador to the United Nations,

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:59.820
at that time there were 183 countries in the
U.N. It was one of the first times that I

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:05.509
didn't have to cook lunch myself and so I
asked my assistant to invite all the women

00:01:05.509 --> 00:01:08.220
permanent representatives to lunch.

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:10.790
And I thought that there would be a lot of
people there.

00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:15.670
When I got there, there were six other women.

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:21.220
Because I'm an American, I formed a caucus
and we called ourselves the G-7 -- [laughter]

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:25.190
-- and we did in fact work together very closely.

00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:29.969
And when I became secretary of state I created
a group of foreign ministers.

00:01:29.969 --> 00:01:33.820
And so at that time there were, like, 14 of
us.

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:35.720
Now there are many, many more.

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:45.539
And so I am very, very happy to be able to
welcome a woman who is part of that group.

00:01:45.539 --> 00:01:50.899
Hina Rabbani Khar is a powerful example of
why having more women in senior government

00:01:50.899 --> 00:01:52.689
positions is so key.

00:01:52.689 --> 00:01:59.520
As the first woman Pakistan foreign minister,
she has joined Benazir Bhutto in blazing a

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:05.320
historic path for women in Pakistan, and she
is taking her country's toughest challenges

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:09.470
at a time of great upheaval and uncertainty
for much of the world.

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:15.620
In a year steeped in heated political rhetoric,
Minister Khar has continued to stress the

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:20.980
critical role that dialogue and diplomacy
can play in resolving regional and global

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:25.390
problems, a sentiment, I think, that many
of us wholeheartedly share.

00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:31.709
We're very fortunate to have this chance to
hear from her concerning her perspective on

00:02:31.709 --> 00:02:36.239
Pakistan-U.S. relations and other topics that
may be on our minds.

00:02:36.239 --> 00:02:43.690
So please join me in greeting our distinguished
foreign minister friend, Hina Rabbani Khar.

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:44.940
[Applause.]

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:53.709
&gt;&gt; FOREIGN MINISTER HINA RABBANI KHAR: [In
Arabic.]

00:02:53.709 --> 00:02:56.270
Good morning and asalaam aleikum to everyone.

00:02:56.270 --> 00:03:01.780
Let me just say that it is truly a matter
of privilege and pleasure for me to be here.

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:07.190
And for Madam Albright, for you to be chairing
it is of course a matter of honor for me.

00:03:07.190 --> 00:03:13.769
And let me acknowledge the role that the CFR
plays in the foreign policy dynamics of your

00:03:13.769 --> 00:03:14.769
country.

00:03:14.769 --> 00:03:19.500
We hope that Pakistan will also have emerging
institutions which can guide us as we move

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:20.500
forward.

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:24.989
Ladies and gentlemen, I would like to take
the opportunity today to be able to have a

00:03:24.989 --> 00:03:29.379
frank and honest discussion because, Madam
Albright, I think increasingly one is finding

00:03:29.379 --> 00:03:33.020
out that in our days now almost everything
is on the record.

00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:37.019
So even when you're off the record, you're
pretty much on the record.

00:03:37.019 --> 00:03:38.189
[Laughter.]

00:03:38.189 --> 00:03:43.640
But as we move forward and we talk about Pakistan-U.S.
relations, whether it's beyond security or

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:49.870
the new dynamics of it and the emerging dynamics
of it and what we are able to achieve together,

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:56.099
I think it is important to first talk about
Pakistan itself because I have often said

00:03:56.099 --> 00:04:03.069
in the last two days that as foreign minister
of Pakistan I find myself talking more about

00:04:03.069 --> 00:04:07.780
what Pakistan is not about rather than what
Pakistan is about.

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:12.879
And I find myself doing that because I think
there are misperceptions about Pakistan, about

00:04:12.879 --> 00:04:18.160
Pakistan's intent, about Pakistan's policy
dynamic, about how Pakistan perceives or what

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:21.040
Pakistan perceives to be in its national interest.

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:24.070
The misperception in that runs supreme.

00:04:24.070 --> 00:04:29.820
It is -- the misperception so much clouds
our real intent and our real intentions and

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:35.310
our real goals and objectives within the region
and within Pakistan that sometimes it seems

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:40.320
as if Pakistan is quite literally on a suicidal
track, if you were to believe all the misperceptions

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:44.789
about Pakistan which are given to it.

00:04:44.789 --> 00:04:51.650
Now, in order to talk about Pakistan, the
potential in Pakistan-U.S. relations, as I

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:56.560
said I think it is important to look at what
Pakistan is today, and even more importantly,

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:00.389
what future Pakistan is marching towards.

00:05:00.389 --> 00:05:05.639
And again, I think I often say that what looks
chaotic to the foreign eye is actually a lot

00:05:05.639 --> 00:05:09.450
of good happening within Pakistan, a lot of
institution-building happening in Pakistan,

00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:14.120
a lot of democracy finding real roots in Pakistan
and democracy starting to deliver.

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:19.000
And I know this question is often asked, how
is democracy delivering in Pakistan, so let

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:22.110
me come straight to that.

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:27.880
This will, inshallah, in the next few months
be the first transfer of power from a civilian

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:33.650
government to a civilian government, under
an election which is held after the 20th amendment

00:05:33.650 --> 00:05:38.560
to the constitution, which empowered and made
more independent the election commission of

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:39.560
Pakistan.

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:46.220
This in itself is going to hopefully create
the irreversibility of democracy in Pakistan,

00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:50.110
and therefore I no more -- I no longer talk
about civilian governments in Pakistan because

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:53.970
there are only one type of government which
are going to be there in Pakistan.

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:55.849
And what has democracy been able to achieve?

00:05:55.849 --> 00:06:00.760
Ladies and gentlemen, in a house which had
a bare majority when it elected its first

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:08.919
prime minister, we have been able to go through
three two-third-majority-requiring constitutional

00:06:08.919 --> 00:06:10.120
amendments.

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:13.729
And one of which we are particularly proud
of is the 18th amendment to the constitution,

00:06:13.729 --> 00:06:20.560
which brought the 1973 constitution, which
came under Zulfikar Ali Bhutto.

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:21.560
And why was it important?

00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:26.840
Because it was a consensus constitution in
Pakistan which brought that back.

00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:33.190
Within that constitution another change was
also made, that not only was it required for

00:06:33.190 --> 00:06:39.270
all children to go to school but it was now
the responsibility of the state that all girl

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:41.770
and boy children must be educated.

00:06:41.770 --> 00:06:46.690
So this is why we have our brawls outside
of the parliament, the political parties in

00:06:46.690 --> 00:06:50.419
Pakistan, but when it comes to matters of
national interest the political parties in

00:06:50.419 --> 00:06:57.620
Pakistan today have matured to come together
and strive for a future that is better.

00:06:57.620 --> 00:07:02.720
What this amendment of the constitution will
also do will correct a lot of historic wrongs

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:05.210
that happened within Pakistan.

00:07:05.210 --> 00:07:10.280
Pakistan was conceived to be a federation,
with the provinces having resources and responsibilities.

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:16.889
Over time, with 30-odd years under dictatorial
regimes, we found more and more centralization

00:07:16.889 --> 00:07:20.370
of power and resources within the center.

00:07:20.370 --> 00:07:24.949
I'm proud to say today that we have decentralized
that, and therefore I don't fear for the future

00:07:24.949 --> 00:07:29.940
of our children any more because the resources
today are where the responsibility to teach

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:35.770
them and to feed them and to clothe them and
to give them health facilities is.

00:07:35.770 --> 00:07:42.810
Also, in all of this chaos, so to speak, Pakistan
is marching forward with institution-building

00:07:42.810 --> 00:07:43.810
like never before.

00:07:43.810 --> 00:07:49.069
And I think an excellent example of that is
the free and independent judiciary which sometimes,

00:07:49.069 --> 00:07:58.129
you know, there is not very helpful in the
sense that we are proud of the fact that despite

00:07:58.129 --> 00:08:04.569
the many challenges that some of these issues,
you know, are faced with, the government is

00:08:04.569 --> 00:08:10.860
faced with, the government has marched on
this track to ensure that we have a free,

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:12.880
independent judicial system in Pakistan.

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:21.349
We also have one of the most interesting and
one of the most free media in -- anywhere

00:08:21.349 --> 00:08:22.349
in the world.

00:08:22.349 --> 00:08:25.500
With 60 channels that are talking mostly about
what government has not been able to do, rather

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:30.599
than what government has been able to do,
I think that also creates a lot of hope for

00:08:30.599 --> 00:08:33.140
the future.

00:08:33.140 --> 00:08:38.610
I'm now going to talk about the two things
which define what democracy has been able

00:08:38.610 --> 00:08:44.450
to do in Pakistan more than anything else,
and the two areas in which I think the results

00:08:44.450 --> 00:08:46.230
are already there.

00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:53.610
One of those areas is the ownership of our
fight against terrorism.

00:08:53.610 --> 00:08:57.500
I don't know how many of you know, but before
this government came into power in 2008, the

00:08:57.500 --> 00:09:02.340
perception in Pakistan, whether you read the,
you know, the electronic media or the print

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:09.160
media or spoke to people, was this was the
Western world's war which was imposed on Pakistan

00:09:09.160 --> 00:09:11.840
and that Pakistan was fighting somebody else's
war.

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:15.620
It was this government, under the leadership
of President Zardari, which came in and gave

00:09:15.620 --> 00:09:22.030
ownership to that war and gave ownership to
that fight, because this was a fight for the

00:09:22.030 --> 00:09:27.561
future of our children, certainly with a future
of stability and peace in the region, but

00:09:27.561 --> 00:09:29.400
for the future of our children.

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:32.090
This was a fight which was killing our children.

00:09:32.090 --> 00:09:34.840
This was a fight which was killing our soldiers.

00:09:34.840 --> 00:09:41.480
I don't know how many of you know, but in
the last 10 years Pakistan has lost more than

00:09:41.480 --> 00:09:47.810
40,000 people altogether, 40,000 lives in
this fight against terrorism.

00:09:47.810 --> 00:09:58.000
In the last five years only we've had almost
352 suicide bomb attacks from 2007 to 2012

00:09:58.000 --> 00:09:59.460
inside Pakistan's territory.

00:09:59.460 --> 00:10:05.400
And allow me to share with you that pre-9/11
we only had one suicide bomb attack inside

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:07.660
Pakistani territory.

00:10:07.660 --> 00:10:11.770
Allow me to share with you that the economic
losses have been colossal, in tens of billions

00:10:11.770 --> 00:10:12.770
of dollars.

00:10:12.770 --> 00:10:18.340
The estimates are ranging between $75 billion
to $100 billion.

00:10:18.340 --> 00:10:24.640
And in all of this, what is most at risk is
a way of life.

00:10:24.640 --> 00:10:29.210
What is most at risk is the type of society
that we want to build.

00:10:29.210 --> 00:10:34.530
And the biggest fight I think that all of
us collectively must continue to fight is

00:10:34.530 --> 00:10:41.940
to fight the mindset which is prevalent amongst
those who try to destroy us.

00:10:41.940 --> 00:10:47.760
And in saying this, let me say that when we
talk about the threat and the dangers and

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:53.660
the challenges that Pakistan faces, maybe
-- and certainly a lot of other countries

00:10:53.660 --> 00:11:00.900
in the region face also -- but the challenges
that Pakistan faces today have their genesis

00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:06.130
in some historical realities, as you said,
Madame Albright, in the unintended consequences

00:11:06.130 --> 00:11:09.110
of the decisions that we take many, many years
back.

00:11:09.110 --> 00:11:15.020
So therefore, ladies and gentlemen, Pakistan
is very, very aware of the decision-making

00:11:15.020 --> 00:11:20.570
of today in how we manage our foreign policy,
how we manage our territory to have consequences

00:11:20.570 --> 00:11:22.290
for the future.

00:11:22.290 --> 00:11:29.210
And allow me to share with you that what is
happening in Pakistan today in some ways is

00:11:29.210 --> 00:11:36.610
a hangover or a leftover from what happened
in our region many, many years back, almost

00:11:36.610 --> 00:11:38.540
three decades back.

00:11:38.540 --> 00:11:44.390
The arms and the ammunition -- first of all,
let's just talk about what happened over there.

00:11:44.390 --> 00:11:51.010
We know that during the Afghan war in the
Soviet era we all collectively -- at least

00:11:51.010 --> 00:11:56.580
this country and mine, at least your country
and mine -- collectively chose to harness

00:11:56.580 --> 00:12:03.220
religious and what was then the jihadi ideology
to fight the Soviets in Afghanistan as an

00:12:03.220 --> 00:12:04.620
instrument of war.

00:12:04.620 --> 00:12:09.040
We did that collectively, but it was done
on our soil.

00:12:09.040 --> 00:12:14.580
The arms and ammunition, the training that
was given was meant for a purpose.

00:12:14.580 --> 00:12:21.740
As the U.S. -- as the strategy succeeded in
Afghanistan, we declared victory and we moved

00:12:21.740 --> 00:12:22.740
on.

00:12:22.740 --> 00:12:30.790
Some of us were able to move on; the others,
like Pakistan, were left over with the mindset,

00:12:30.790 --> 00:12:36.170
with the training, with the arms, with the
ammunition, with the bombs, with the (Kalashnikovs

00:12:36.170 --> 00:12:42.700
?), with the narcotics trade, and still today
after three decades we are still suffering

00:12:42.700 --> 00:12:43.910
from each one of those.

00:12:43.910 --> 00:12:46.900
We are suffering the consequences of each
one of those.

00:12:46.900 --> 00:12:52.460
And to me the biggest challenge in all of
this is the mindset that it inculcated, how

00:12:52.460 --> 00:12:58.610
it has destroyed our society, how it faces
us as the biggest challenge that we have to

00:12:58.610 --> 00:12:59.910
face.

00:12:59.910 --> 00:13:06.120
Ladies and gentlemen, in this, from the lessons
that we have learned from the past I think

00:13:06.120 --> 00:13:12.440
it is important to apply some of those lessons
as we look at the future.

00:13:12.440 --> 00:13:17.260
As we look at the challenges that we face
within the region, Pakistan has grave concerns

00:13:17.260 --> 00:13:23.110
over what is going to happen in the future.

00:13:23.110 --> 00:13:28.670
We are -- again, I think because I am going
to go and talk a bit about our relations with

00:13:28.670 --> 00:13:33.690
Afghanistan and how we view Afghanistan, and
later maybe about our relations with Afghanistan,

00:13:33.690 --> 00:13:36.450
allow me to say what we do not want for Afghanistan.

00:13:36.450 --> 00:13:41.180
Because as I said, it is not important for
me to say what we do want for Afghanistan.

00:13:41.180 --> 00:13:45.250
But what is it that we do not want, which
is particularly -- which is typically ascribed

00:13:45.250 --> 00:13:46.250
to Pakistan?

00:13:46.250 --> 00:13:53.740
Ladies and gentlemen, first of all let me
say that it is not by -- it is not ambition

00:13:53.740 --> 00:13:57.770
but anxiety which drives our interest in Afghanistan.

00:13:57.770 --> 00:14:03.060
Allow me to say that we have no favorites
in terms of ethnicities, in terms of groups.

00:14:03.060 --> 00:14:09.320
Pakistan today is on a track to develop relations
with Afghanistan as a sovereign equal, and

00:14:09.320 --> 00:14:15.880
this to me is a big achievement of the democratic
government in Pakistan.

00:14:15.880 --> 00:14:25.050
Pakistan today fears for instability in Afghanistan
because instability from Afghanistan permeates

00:14:25.050 --> 00:14:31.740
through the 2,000-plus kilometer border that
we have with Afghanistan directly into Pakistan's

00:14:31.740 --> 00:14:34.350
territory, as it has for the last three decades.

00:14:34.350 --> 00:14:39.280
There are 53,000 people which cross the Pakistan-Afghan
border every day.

00:14:39.280 --> 00:14:45.280
So you can imagine the permeation and the
instantaneous effects of any instability in

00:14:45.280 --> 00:14:47.430
Afghanistan.

00:14:47.430 --> 00:14:50.230
Allow me to say that we seek no strategic
depth in Afghanistan.

00:14:50.230 --> 00:14:52.180
Allow me to categorically say that.

00:14:52.180 --> 00:14:57.170
The only strategic depth that we seek with
Afghanistan is friendly; not even will we

00:14:57.170 --> 00:15:03.120
seek relations with Afghanistan which are
based on a stable -- on the principle of stable,

00:15:03.120 --> 00:15:08.320
peaceful Afghanistan, a sovereign Afghanistan,
an independent Afghanistan.

00:15:08.320 --> 00:15:15.000
We have no intention nor any national interest
to impose any type of government in Afghanistan.

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:17.120
It is for the Afghans to do so.

00:15:17.120 --> 00:15:22.060
Why all of this is ascribed to Pakistan typically
is because, as I said before, because of the

00:15:22.060 --> 00:15:23.790
hangover of the past.

00:15:23.790 --> 00:15:27.930
And I think it is important that we are able
to unload the past and move into the future

00:15:27.930 --> 00:15:35.500
because I fear that if we are not able to
do that, we will see ourselves inadvertently

00:15:35.500 --> 00:15:38.230
repeating the same mistakes.

00:15:38.230 --> 00:15:45.540
It will be the consequences of not realizing
reality as it existed.

00:15:45.540 --> 00:15:50.010
Now if we look at -- since I was talking about
the ownership of the war on terrorism, and

00:15:50.010 --> 00:15:54.850
if we look at the challenges that Pakistan
faces today, very strongly because by no means

00:15:54.850 --> 00:16:04.130
we can underestimate them, we are of course
very, very concerned about the state because

00:16:04.130 --> 00:16:09.320
we know that the entry goals in Afghanistan
were very, very different from what is appearing

00:16:09.320 --> 00:16:13.110
to be now the transition goals.

00:16:13.110 --> 00:16:19.800
What we want from the world, what we want
from the 49 nations which are operating in

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:26.650
Afghanistan is that there is no security vacuum
left behind as we go through an exit.

00:16:26.650 --> 00:16:31.330
Ladies and gentlemen, for us a few barometers
by which we would look at what is happening

00:16:31.330 --> 00:16:34.060
in Afghanistan are the following.

00:16:34.060 --> 00:16:41.910
One, until about 2007 we saw a stream, a small
stream but a stream of more than 5 million

00:16:41.910 --> 00:16:45.140
Afghan refugees that live in Pakistan going
back into Afghanistan.

00:16:45.140 --> 00:16:52.390
Since then, especially since 2009, we almost
see a reverse trend back.

00:16:52.390 --> 00:16:56.800
That to me does not bear a lot of confidence.

00:16:56.800 --> 00:17:05.310
That to me does not look like we are achieving
the goals that we set out for ourselves collectively.

00:17:05.310 --> 00:17:12.140
Recently the incidents that we've had of green-on-blue
attacks and the decision not to go through

00:17:12.140 --> 00:17:16.800
joint training, these are all huge concerns
for Pakistan.

00:17:16.800 --> 00:17:22.100
These are huge concerns because we will not
be able to change our location.

00:17:22.100 --> 00:17:25.050
We will not be able to change our geography.

00:17:25.050 --> 00:17:33.050
Because we must make sure that the security
situation in Afghanistan is good enough for

00:17:33.050 --> 00:17:39.470
us to be able to build a peaceful and stable
neighborhood.

00:17:39.470 --> 00:17:44.910
Recently the third barometer, recently from
the area of -- [inaudible] -- there has been

00:17:44.910 --> 00:17:48.910
infiltration in Pakistan territory where hundreds
and two hundreds of militants come inside

00:17:48.910 --> 00:17:55.040
Pakistan territory, and as recently as three
weeks back they beheaded, slaughtered 70 of

00:17:55.040 --> 00:17:56.910
our soldiers.

00:17:56.910 --> 00:18:04.450
All of these are not signs which inspire a
lot of confidence in the security situation

00:18:04.450 --> 00:18:16.910
in Afghanistan, and we fear that through the
long border we will have to face more challenges

00:18:16.910 --> 00:18:19.720
in the future.

00:18:19.720 --> 00:18:25.540
As we move forward, it is important that we
are able to get out of the trust deficit mantra

00:18:25.540 --> 00:18:31.250
because, as I say, I have said what is in
Pakistan's national interest and I have said

00:18:31.250 --> 00:18:32.940
what is not in Pakistan's national interest.

00:18:32.940 --> 00:18:37.910
And allow me to say that what I consider today
to be the primary goal national interest of

00:18:37.910 --> 00:18:44.240
Pakistan is a peaceful and stable -- not necessarily
even friendly -- but a peaceful and stable

00:18:44.240 --> 00:18:45.240
Afghanistan.

00:18:45.240 --> 00:18:48.570
Because we know from history that until and
unless there is peace and stability in Afghanistan,

00:18:48.570 --> 00:18:51.610
we will not be able to find our peace and
stability.

00:18:51.610 --> 00:18:54.860
And we will not be able to grow economically
the way we wish.

00:18:54.860 --> 00:19:03.270
We will not be able to achieve the social
goals for our children that we wish to achieve.

00:19:03.270 --> 00:19:06.930
So it is -- we consider it to be in our core
national interest to have a peaceful, stable

00:19:06.930 --> 00:19:11.350
Afghanistan and it is time that we put all
our energy together to be able to achieve

00:19:11.350 --> 00:19:12.690
those ends.

00:19:12.690 --> 00:19:16.670
What is the best possible scenario that we
can think of in 2014?

00:19:16.670 --> 00:19:21.920
The best possible scenario that we can think
of for 2014 is that as elections take place

00:19:21.920 --> 00:19:27.450
and as transition takes place in Afghanistan,
all Afghan groups are able to demonstrate

00:19:27.450 --> 00:19:36.519
their strength, their will through the election
process, not through violence.

00:19:36.519 --> 00:19:39.340
And that is a future that we must be working
toward.

00:19:39.340 --> 00:19:47.290
That is the immediate short-term future that
we must be working toward.

00:19:47.290 --> 00:19:52.690
I'm just going to end by talking a bit about
how democracy has delivered within the region.

00:19:52.690 --> 00:19:54.570
I talked to you about our relations with Afghanistan.

00:19:54.570 --> 00:19:56.560
Our relations with Afghanistan have not been
easy.

00:19:56.560 --> 00:19:58.440
I'm the first one to admit them.

00:19:58.440 --> 00:20:02.120
But I want you to consider on what Pakistan
is trying to do with Afghanistan.

00:20:02.120 --> 00:20:10.280
When President Zardari was elected as president,
he only invited one foreigner to his inaugural,

00:20:10.280 --> 00:20:13.560
and that foreigner happened to be President
Karzai.

00:20:13.560 --> 00:20:16.260
In doing that he was sending an important
message.

00:20:16.260 --> 00:20:22.510
He was sending a message of respect, a message
of treating each other as sovereign equals.

00:20:22.510 --> 00:20:28.130
And he was sending another very important
message, that to Pakistan today the most important

00:20:28.130 --> 00:20:34.890
capital in the world is possibly not Washington,
D.C. or London or Berlin.

00:20:34.890 --> 00:20:40.830
It is Kabul, because it is in Kabul that our
future is interlinked.

00:20:40.830 --> 00:20:46.400
We have suffered through 30 years of instability
and we have seen the negative externality

00:20:46.400 --> 00:20:47.670
of combat.

00:20:47.670 --> 00:20:52.950
We hope to suffer from the positive externality
or benefit from the positive externally, from

00:20:52.950 --> 00:20:56.410
pace and stability in Afghanistan in the next
three decades.

00:20:56.410 --> 00:21:02.860
So we have a real interest in ensuring that
there is stability in Afghanistan.

00:21:02.860 --> 00:21:09.310
The other area that I want you to concentrate
on is what Pakistan is trying to achieve within

00:21:09.310 --> 00:21:13.680
the region, what type of relations it is pursuing
within the region.

00:21:13.680 --> 00:21:19.150
Because one thing that is clear to us is that
we will not be able to see peace within if

00:21:19.150 --> 00:21:24.450
we do not find peace on our boundaries, on
our borders with our neighbors.

00:21:24.450 --> 00:21:30.310
And therefore, ladies and gentlemen, the change
in mindset of approach to a neighbor which

00:21:30.310 --> 00:21:36.820
which we've had a particularly hostile relation,
for almost 65 years of our history we have

00:21:36.820 --> 00:21:42.570
built on the hostile, negative narrative with
India and they have done the same with Pakistan.

00:21:42.570 --> 00:21:46.670
We've all done a fantastic -- both the countries,
done a fantastic job at it.

00:21:46.670 --> 00:21:49.870
And we have our issues with India, serious
issues.

00:21:49.870 --> 00:21:52.620
The core issue of Jammu and Kashmir is a serious
problem.

00:21:52.620 --> 00:21:54.880
It is not a figment of anyone's imagination.

00:21:54.880 --> 00:21:59.700
The disputes, the territorial disputes on
Siachen, on -- [inaudible] -- Creek are also

00:21:59.700 --> 00:22:00.910
serious problems.

00:22:00.910 --> 00:22:04.220
However, what is the way to be able to solve
those problems?

00:22:04.220 --> 00:22:05.980
Is it through wars?

00:22:05.980 --> 00:22:10.880
Is it through creating a narrative of more
hostility and animosity and filling the minds

00:22:10.880 --> 00:22:16.400
of our next generation with the same narrative
that we did of the previous generation?

00:22:16.400 --> 00:22:20.110
I think the answer to that is a clear and
simple no.

00:22:20.110 --> 00:22:24.860
The way to solve these issues is by building
trust, is by changing the mindset.

00:22:24.860 --> 00:22:29.320
Is by building enough trust to be able to
sit across the negotiating table and talk

00:22:29.320 --> 00:22:33.640
through these issues and find lasting solutions.

00:22:33.640 --> 00:22:38.490
And I will not underestimate the importance
of finding lasting solutions because until

00:22:38.490 --> 00:22:42.340
and unless we are able to find lasting solutions
to these issues that we have in India, we

00:22:42.340 --> 00:22:46.490
will not be able to disarm the nay-sayers.

00:22:46.490 --> 00:22:52.740
So in the last four years what you have seen
is this government pursuing single-mindedly

00:22:52.740 --> 00:22:58.750
a track of reaching out to our east, meaning
India, to our west, to Afghanistan, to treating

00:22:58.750 --> 00:23:02.530
each other as sovereign equals with respect.

00:23:02.530 --> 00:23:08.360
And in doing that, the best message that we
sent India's way is changing a decision that

00:23:08.360 --> 00:23:11.000
we had taken and stuck to for almost 40 years.

00:23:11.000 --> 00:23:15.020
We said we will not normalize trade with India.

00:23:15.020 --> 00:23:19.850
And this government was able to send a message
that we will normalize trade with India.

00:23:19.850 --> 00:23:24.020
We will normalize trade with India because
it is important for the economic integration

00:23:24.020 --> 00:23:28.700
and trade and investment in each other's countries,
but even more importantly it is important

00:23:28.700 --> 00:23:33.630
because we have to invest in building stakeholders
in each other's future, because we have to

00:23:33.630 --> 00:23:39.590
start looking at ourselves from a regional
lens and not separate lenses, and it is no

00:23:39.590 --> 00:23:45.090
mystery and it is not unknown to any one of
us that it is regions which have emerged -- whether

00:23:45.090 --> 00:23:49.220
you look at ASEAN, whether you look at the
European Union, whether you look at South

00:23:49.220 --> 00:23:53.790
America -- it is regions which have emerged
economically and otherwise.

00:23:53.790 --> 00:24:01.840
So let's just, you know, go to what we feel
is important as we move forward within our

00:24:01.840 --> 00:24:07.940
relations with the U.S. A lot is said about
what doesn't work with Pakistan-U.S. relations.

00:24:07.940 --> 00:24:12.710
I think a lot less is said about what has
worked for 60 years.

00:24:12.710 --> 00:24:18.350
A lot less is said about the fact that the
U.S., for instance, happens to be an investor

00:24:18.350 --> 00:24:20.010
in the future of Pakistan.

00:24:20.010 --> 00:24:25.520
The alma mater where I come from, Lahore University
of Management Sciences, which I'm proud to

00:24:25.520 --> 00:24:29.000
have been a student of, had contributions
from USAID.

00:24:29.000 --> 00:24:31.170
And believe me, I went to your colleges also.

00:24:31.170 --> 00:24:33.200
LUMS is actually almost better.

00:24:33.200 --> 00:24:37.350
So what you helped us create is sometimes
better than some of your own colleges, and

00:24:37.350 --> 00:24:42.310
that is the type of contribution that we want,
which is sustainable, which is there to stay,

00:24:42.310 --> 00:24:46.200
which is there to be taken on by Pakistani
institutions.

00:24:46.200 --> 00:24:52.000
As we move forward we must also understand
that, you know, at least for Pakistan's sake

00:24:52.000 --> 00:24:56.460
we are a country with -- you happen to be
our largest trading partner.

00:24:56.460 --> 00:25:00.059
You almost happen to be the largest investors
in Pakistan.

00:25:00.059 --> 00:25:02.230
There are many things which unite us.

00:25:02.230 --> 00:25:05.910
There are many things in which we are stakeholders
in each other's future.

00:25:05.910 --> 00:25:13.150
But most importantly, I think without any
doubt what we can achieve together within

00:25:13.150 --> 00:25:17.380
the region I feel we have not done justice
to.

00:25:17.380 --> 00:25:22.640
I feel that we have let the talk of trust
deficit overwhelm us.

00:25:22.640 --> 00:25:25.130
I feel we have not given ourselves a chance.

00:25:25.130 --> 00:25:28.620
Allow me to say that if the stated objective
of the United States of America for their

00:25:28.620 --> 00:25:33.620
troop presence in Afghanistan is peace and
stability in Afghanistan, then you are pursuing

00:25:33.620 --> 00:25:39.430
the national interest of Pakistan, because
the stated and the known and known objective

00:25:39.430 --> 00:25:42.810
of Pakistan within the region is also peace
and stability.

00:25:42.810 --> 00:25:47.020
Why I talked about the regional approach was
to give you confidence that we are actually

00:25:47.020 --> 00:25:53.360
walking the talk of pursuing that, that you
must not look at us from a 30-year-old lens.

00:25:53.360 --> 00:25:58.400
That you must look at us from the lens of
today, and the lens of today tells you clearly

00:25:58.400 --> 00:26:05.420
that the future that Pakistan wants for itself
is that of peace and stability.

00:26:05.420 --> 00:26:11.010
And until and when we have a peaceful, stable
region I think everything else, Madame Albright,

00:26:11.010 --> 00:26:12.030
will follow.

00:26:12.030 --> 00:26:18.150
So as we march on on a track with the U.S.,
I think we need some -- we need to send each

00:26:18.150 --> 00:26:23.150
other messages, and one of the main purpose
of my trip here is to send that message loud

00:26:23.150 --> 00:26:30.510
and clear, that we are keen to work together
to ensure that in Afghanistan we are able

00:26:30.510 --> 00:26:37.920
to achieve together long, sustainable peace
-- let's admit it -- that we were not able

00:26:37.920 --> 00:26:40.940
to achieve 30 years back.

00:26:40.940 --> 00:26:43.190
So let's work together.

00:26:43.190 --> 00:26:45.240
Let's put our energies together.

00:26:45.240 --> 00:26:47.050
Let's put our resources together.

00:26:47.050 --> 00:26:49.340
Let's not waste time in blaming each other.

00:26:49.340 --> 00:26:56.300
Let's find time to sit together and work through
this together because, as I said, this is

00:26:56.300 --> 00:26:58.800
something which is of primary national interest.

00:26:58.800 --> 00:27:03.250
So in this relation I think what do the Pakistani
people look for?

00:27:03.250 --> 00:27:09.741
The Pakistani people, before anything else,
look for respect, look to be treated as a

00:27:09.741 --> 00:27:11.430
sovereign equal.

00:27:11.430 --> 00:27:17.890
And this is the respect that we are striving
to ensure is clearly demonstrated in our relations

00:27:17.890 --> 00:27:19.420
with Afghanistan also.

00:27:19.420 --> 00:27:22.510
We might be the biggest country, we might
be the more resourceful country, we might

00:27:22.510 --> 00:27:27.090
be the country with better institutions, but
we must -- for Pakistan I'm speaking -- treat

00:27:27.090 --> 00:27:29.140
Afghanistan as a sovereign equal.

00:27:29.140 --> 00:27:32.520
And that is what will inspire confidence in
the Afghans.

00:27:32.520 --> 00:27:36.930
That is what will give us our strategic depth
in Afghanistan.

00:27:36.930 --> 00:27:41.630
So in the same way when you look at Pakistan-U.S.
relations, I think it is important that we

00:27:41.630 --> 00:27:45.700
are able to inspire confidence amongst Pakistanis.

00:27:45.700 --> 00:27:49.850
And one thing that does come in the way of
us being able to inspire confidence in Pakistanis

00:27:49.850 --> 00:27:53.179
is the use of unilateral strikes such as drone
strikes.

00:27:53.179 --> 00:27:57.020
Because what that does is, being illegal,
being counter-productive -- most importantly

00:27:57.020 --> 00:28:02.860
what it does is that it makes it again your
war and not our war.

00:28:02.860 --> 00:28:08.020
It is important that this remain something
that the Pakistanis are striving for for their

00:28:08.020 --> 00:28:13.110
own future, and that the Pakistanis should
be allowed to look at it that way.

00:28:13.110 --> 00:28:20.270
We expect from the U.S. a deeper understanding
of Pakistan's priorities, Pakistan's concerns,

00:28:20.270 --> 00:28:25.470
Pakistan's constraints also because I think
sometimes we are given an overload of capacity.

00:28:25.470 --> 00:28:30.850
We are expected to achieve on our side what
49 other countries cannot achieve on the other

00:28:30.850 --> 00:28:31.850
side.

00:28:31.850 --> 00:28:37.429
So how big do you think we are, or how resourceful
do you think we are?

00:28:37.429 --> 00:28:44.750
And most importantly, I think we have of course
always been very grateful for the very strong

00:28:44.750 --> 00:28:51.540
message that came from the American people
when you supported the democracy in Pakistan

00:28:51.540 --> 00:28:58.020
by again, Senator Kerry, Secretary Clinton
of course had a huge role to play in it.

00:28:58.020 --> 00:29:01.590
The KLB was important, the Kerry-Lugar bill
was an important milestone.

00:29:01.590 --> 00:29:06.230
It sent a clear message that the U.S. wants
to build long-sustainable relations.

00:29:06.230 --> 00:29:13.440
We also feel that a clearer message still
would be recognizing that Pakistan is a country

00:29:13.440 --> 00:29:18.350
which has gone through many, many challenges
and therefore a preferential market access

00:29:18.350 --> 00:29:20.910
for Pakistani products into U.S. markets.

00:29:20.910 --> 00:29:24.220
What that will do is that it will build new
stakeholders in Pakistan.

00:29:24.220 --> 00:29:28.350
What that will do is that it will immediately
give the people who today use violence as

00:29:28.350 --> 00:29:33.860
means of expressing themselves, it will give
them an alternative.

00:29:33.860 --> 00:29:39.700
Really, at the end of the day it is all about
providing alternatives in the type of region

00:29:39.700 --> 00:29:40.840
that I come from.

00:29:40.840 --> 00:29:45.740
So with those words let me just say that I
hope we have a more interesting discussion

00:29:45.740 --> 00:29:53.620
still, and it's been a pleasure, and we hope
that the biggest challenge in front of us

00:29:53.620 --> 00:29:57.760
today is what we can achieve together in Afghanistan.

00:29:57.760 --> 00:30:03.870
And as we strive towards that, I think in
building these relations we hope that Afghanistan

00:30:03.870 --> 00:30:14.250
will not be left in a security -- with 
a security vacuum.

00:30:14.250 --> 00:30:15.250
[Applause.]

00:30:15.250 --> 00:30:21.400
&gt;&gt; ALBRIGHT: Well, thank you very, very much
for that very clear and full statement.

00:30:21.400 --> 00:30:23.429
I am not a journalist.

00:30:23.429 --> 00:30:26.100
I actually had the same job you have.

00:30:26.100 --> 00:30:36.010
And I know what it's like to come to another
country and be subjected to an elite opinion-maker

00:30:36.010 --> 00:30:38.330
audience.

00:30:38.330 --> 00:30:42.520
And so I -- what I want to get at are some
of the questions of what it's like to actually

00:30:42.520 --> 00:30:45.520
carry out foreign policy for your country.

00:30:45.520 --> 00:30:50.370
But I can't resist -- you made a lot of news
yesterday.

00:30:50.370 --> 00:30:57.560
And among the things you talked about was
the fact that you revealed that there soon

00:30:57.560 --> 00:31:01.450
would be confidential talks with the United
States in Afghanistan.

00:31:01.450 --> 00:31:05.600
So could you tell us what you're going to
talk about in these confidential talks?

00:31:05.600 --> 00:31:06.740
(Laughter.)

00:31:06.740 --> 00:31:10.169
&gt;&gt; KHAR: As I said, nothing nowadays is off
the record.

00:31:10.169 --> 00:31:11.169
&gt;&gt; ALBRIGHT: (Laughs.)

00:31:11.169 --> 00:31:14.460
&gt;&gt; KHAR: But, you know, as I said, I think
-- I think we are truly -- I'm glad that I'm

00:31:14.460 --> 00:31:15.770
here when I am.

00:31:15.770 --> 00:31:22.610
I wouldn't have wanted to be in Washington
in the last -- before we were able to move

00:31:22.610 --> 00:31:25.190
back on rebuilding the relations.

00:31:25.190 --> 00:31:30.700
So I feel that today we are in some ways at
a turning point.

00:31:30.700 --> 00:31:35.250
I think we have all learned our lessons of
trying to do in Afghanistan what we think

00:31:35.250 --> 00:31:36.730
we could achieve.

00:31:36.730 --> 00:31:41.190
I think it is important that we are able to
put our energies together, as I said, in the

00:31:41.190 --> 00:31:46.730
talks and move to see what we can achieve
together in Afghanistan.

00:31:46.730 --> 00:31:53.800
And for that, we already have a system by
which Afghanistan, Pakistan and U.S., a core

00:31:53.800 --> 00:32:00.340
group -- in the form of a core group at the
level of the foreign secretaries -- meet.

00:32:00.340 --> 00:32:06.860
And they talk about whatever areas and whatever
parts to follow in order to achieve peace

00:32:06.860 --> 00:32:07.860
and stability.

00:32:07.860 --> 00:32:11.570
For that, of course, reconciliation is a major
element of that.

00:32:11.570 --> 00:32:18.960
So we feel that with -- ties with the U.S.
on a positive trajectory, that we have been

00:32:18.960 --> 00:32:24.490
able to build enough confidence in each other
to start those serious discussions.

00:32:24.490 --> 00:32:28.670
And Madam Albright, the concerns that we have,
of course, are the type that I talked about,

00:32:28.670 --> 00:32:33.160
that we may leave behind a security vacuum,
which will come back to haunt us as the one

00:32:33.160 --> 00:32:37.390
we left behind three decades back is still
haunting Pakistan.

00:32:37.390 --> 00:32:45.750
So it's important that as we look towards
2014 we are able to build an environment whereby

00:32:45.750 --> 00:32:53.660
all of one people feel they can express themselves
through the political process.

00:32:53.660 --> 00:32:58.619
And that is what we will do whatever we can
to assist our Afghan brothers and sisters.

00:32:58.619 --> 00:33:01.460
&gt;&gt; ALBRIGHT: And are they going to begin now
the talks or --

00:33:01.460 --> 00:33:05.100
&gt;&gt; KHAR: I think in some ways, you know, this
was already a work in progress, but we have

00:33:05.100 --> 00:33:09.750
a much bigger opportunity today than we had,
you know, a few months back to be able to

00:33:09.750 --> 00:33:10.750
move on this track.

00:33:10.750 --> 00:33:14.100
&gt;&gt; ALBRIGHT: By the way, I'm going to ask
a few questions and then turn it all over

00:33:14.100 --> 00:33:15.410
to you.

00:33:15.410 --> 00:33:16.620
Let me just say this.

00:33:16.620 --> 00:33:23.550
I think that -- first of all, I am among those
Americans who recognize the fact that more

00:33:23.550 --> 00:33:29.510
Pakistanis have died from terrorism than Americans
have died or other parts of the world.

00:33:29.510 --> 00:33:35.950
The question, I think, that people have is
why is Pakistan not fighting terrorism more

00:33:35.950 --> 00:33:36.950
strongly.

00:33:36.950 --> 00:33:41.900
Where do we diverge on the methods for dealing
with terrorists?

00:33:41.900 --> 00:33:47.340
And why are -- why do we misunderstand each
other so much?

00:33:47.340 --> 00:33:48.630
Because I think we do.

00:33:48.630 --> 00:33:55.320
I think one of the issues, from my perspective,
is that you said it was very important for

00:33:55.320 --> 00:33:56.320
respect.

00:33:56.320 --> 00:34:01.610
And yet the sense in following events in Pakistan
and some of the statements is that you see

00:34:01.610 --> 00:34:04.920
yourself as a victim, a country that's a victim.

00:34:04.920 --> 00:34:11.210
And I just wondered where that difference
comes and whether it is because we see different

00:34:11.210 --> 00:34:12.970
methods of fighting terrorism.

00:34:12.970 --> 00:34:14.520
&gt;&gt; KHAR: OK.

00:34:14.520 --> 00:34:20.030
First of all, why Pakistan has not done enough
is a question that surprises me.

00:34:20.030 --> 00:34:24.820
And I think this is where the genesis or the
start of the difference of viewing what we

00:34:24.820 --> 00:34:27.080
have done for each other comes from.

00:34:27.080 --> 00:34:30.470
You know, I said -- I should have said this
from the podium also.

00:34:30.470 --> 00:34:36.600
When I said that this government was able
to take ownership of the fight against terrorism,

00:34:36.600 --> 00:34:41.070
one thing that I forgot to share with you
was that when this government came into power,

00:34:41.070 --> 00:34:47.609
the total area of FATA that was under government's
control was 37 percent.

00:34:47.609 --> 00:34:51.020
Today it is 86 percent.

00:34:51.020 --> 00:34:55.629
We had to do two major military operations
and many, many other operations that continue

00:34:55.629 --> 00:35:00.180
to happen in Pakistan outside of the headlines
that make it to the -- in Washington Post

00:35:00.180 --> 00:35:01.180
or New York Times.

00:35:01.180 --> 00:35:06.010
They continue to happen in Pakistan as we
speak, to ensure that we have more and more

00:35:06.010 --> 00:35:08.300
area which is completely and directly under
our control.

00:35:08.300 --> 00:35:14.690
We feel that the fight we have fought together
against al-Qaida has been relatively successful.

00:35:14.690 --> 00:35:19.430
We feel that we have worked together to get
many al-Qaida operators outside -- out of

00:35:19.430 --> 00:35:20.660
Pakistan.

00:35:20.660 --> 00:35:22.730
So these are things that we have been able
to achieve.

00:35:22.730 --> 00:35:27.200
But however, unfortunately, these are things
which are not acknowledged very often.

00:35:27.200 --> 00:35:34.920
And we are continuing to find ways and means
of improving our capacity to be able to deal

00:35:34.920 --> 00:35:37.260
with this menace as we speak.

00:35:37.260 --> 00:35:43.590
And for -- in Pakistan's case, I think mostly
it is a matter of capacity more than intent.

00:35:43.590 --> 00:35:44.810
The intent is clearly there.

00:35:44.810 --> 00:35:49.310
And that's why my surprise -- because the
intent is demonstrated from the fact that

00:35:49.310 --> 00:35:55.180
who did 6,000 of our soldiers die fighting
against if they were not fighting the terrorists?

00:35:55.180 --> 00:36:00.440
If we were not doing enough, then how did
6,000 of our soldiers die fighting -- for

00:36:00.440 --> 00:36:02.190
what cause were they fighting?

00:36:02.190 --> 00:36:09.810
So really I think what is -- what is -- what
is sometimes incorrectly perceived is this

00:36:09.810 --> 00:36:16.020
over-projection of capacity on Pakistan's
part to be able to do everything at the same

00:36:16.020 --> 00:36:17.090
time.

00:36:17.090 --> 00:36:20.950
If in the last four years we have been able
to achieve what we have against terrorism,

00:36:20.950 --> 00:36:23.980
I think that is clearly a very, very good
starting point.

00:36:23.980 --> 00:36:27.820
And there is clearly more to be done, but
more to be done on two tracks.

00:36:27.820 --> 00:36:31.880
One is certainly the operations that you'll
have -- you'll do, you know, whether at the

00:36:31.880 --> 00:36:34.550
law enforcement agency level or any other
level.

00:36:34.550 --> 00:36:39.010
But the other important one is also be able
to change the mindset.

00:36:39.010 --> 00:36:45.380
And for that, the increasing divide between
the West and the East has to stop.

00:36:45.380 --> 00:36:51.430
And we, therefore, feel that we are doing
our part in being able to project that within

00:36:51.430 --> 00:36:54.400
Pakistan also.

00:36:54.400 --> 00:36:56.970
Then you talked about why the misunderstanding.

00:36:56.970 --> 00:37:01.100
So I think some of the answers to that I've
already given.

00:37:01.100 --> 00:37:05.850
Madam Albright, I'm -- it's interesting that
you talk about a choice between being the

00:37:05.850 --> 00:37:09.240
victim and deserving respect.

00:37:09.240 --> 00:37:14.619
So when the United States went through 9/11,
the United States was a victim of terrorism.

00:37:14.619 --> 00:37:16.250
Your people suffered.

00:37:16.250 --> 00:37:17.340
Your people died.

00:37:17.340 --> 00:37:18.790
You were a victim.

00:37:18.790 --> 00:37:20.980
But you still deserved respect.

00:37:20.980 --> 00:37:22.380
Pakistan wants exactly the same thing.

00:37:22.380 --> 00:37:24.880
Thirty-three thousand of our civilians have
died.

00:37:24.880 --> 00:37:30.609
More than 6,000 of our law enforcement agency
police, military have died, have sacrificed

00:37:30.609 --> 00:37:36.570
their lives willingly -- civilians unwillingly,
of course -- people like myself who are indeed

00:37:36.570 --> 00:37:42.910
from the elite of Pakistan -- our lifestyles
have changed.

00:37:42.910 --> 00:37:46.510
Imagine what it is for the less-privileged
in Pakistan.

00:37:46.510 --> 00:37:50.520
The way we have been able to conduct our lives
has changed.

00:37:50.520 --> 00:37:52.460
Imagine what it is for the less privileged.

00:37:52.460 --> 00:37:55.300
Imagine what it is for the houses of the 6,000
soldiers who have died.

00:37:55.300 --> 00:38:01.480
You think any country -- so we say that ask
of us not what you are not willing to give

00:38:01.480 --> 00:38:02.540
to everyone.

00:38:02.540 --> 00:38:05.410
And look at it as a collective effort.

00:38:05.410 --> 00:38:07.390
Look at it as a collective responsibility.

00:38:07.390 --> 00:38:12.490
When you look at it as a collective responsibility,
you are able to understand each other's capacity

00:38:12.490 --> 00:38:17.100
issues and enhance each other's capacity rather
than blaming each other.

00:38:17.100 --> 00:38:25.310
I think we have lost more at the hands of
the -- you know, the trust deficit, quite

00:38:25.310 --> 00:38:30.180
frankly speaking, and doubting each other's
intentions than we have at the hands of anything

00:38:30.180 --> 00:38:31.180
else.

00:38:31.180 --> 00:38:34.091
So, as I said, we are the victims.

00:38:34.091 --> 00:38:35.570
We don't want to play the victim.

00:38:35.570 --> 00:38:37.630
We don't want to be the victims in the future.

00:38:37.630 --> 00:38:40.030
So for that, we need to work together.

00:38:40.030 --> 00:38:48.050
And I think despite suffering from this very,
very big menace, we certainly do deserve respect

00:38:48.050 --> 00:38:53.839
for each other's sacrifices and respect for
the future that we're trying to build together.

00:38:53.839 --> 00:38:56.750
\
&gt;&gt; ALBRIGHT: Let me ask to elaborate a little

00:38:56.750 --> 00:39:02.140
bit, because I kind of meant more victim in
terms of not having control over your own

00:39:02.140 --> 00:39:03.140
policy.

00:39:03.140 --> 00:39:11.370
And one of the things that -- when I was secretary,
I obviously had to consult with the national

00:39:11.370 --> 00:39:17.510
security adviser, the Pentagon, the secretary
of Treasury -- it's a decision-making process

00:39:17.510 --> 00:39:20.970
-- but ultimately one is part of the decision-making
process.

00:39:20.970 --> 00:39:26.350
I wondered -- for instance, you as foreign
minister -- how does it work within your decision-making

00:39:26.350 --> 00:39:34.440
process so that you are able to feel that
Pakistan is a partner in a lot of these discussions

00:39:34.440 --> 00:39:40.380
so that in fact not -- certainly you are a
victim of terrorism, but I kind of meant victim

00:39:40.380 --> 00:39:45.830
of other countries telling you what to do
in your foreign policy and national security

00:39:45.830 --> 00:39:46.830
policy.

00:39:46.830 --> 00:39:51.780
And then one addendum to that would be is,
we can sit here -- or you will be meeting

00:39:51.780 --> 00:39:56.550
with Secretary Clinton, and you can work out
all kinds of things that you might have in

00:39:56.550 --> 00:40:01.670
common, but there are events that happen that
are difficult to absorb.

00:40:01.670 --> 00:40:07.650
What is the mechanism, or can we all think
about developing a mechanism that deals with

00:40:07.650 --> 00:40:13.580
the crisis that makes -- that brings questions
into the decision-making process and also

00:40:13.580 --> 00:40:19.020
allows us to have that phone call, to say,
OK, we really do trust each other?

00:40:19.020 --> 00:40:20.090
&gt;&gt; KHAR: OK.

00:40:20.090 --> 00:40:23.940
First of all, the decision-making process
you talked about -- and you talked about how

00:40:23.940 --> 00:40:28.109
as secretary you had to consult and get inputs
-- I think it's pretty much the same.

00:40:28.109 --> 00:40:33.090
So this -- if you were alluding to the military
in Pakistan, allow me to say this -- and I'm

00:40:33.090 --> 00:40:37.090
saying this on the record, off the record
-- and I would say this, as I said, on the

00:40:37.090 --> 00:40:41.130
record and off the record -- the military
in Pakistan has no more a role than the Pentagon

00:40:41.130 --> 00:40:42.940
in the U.S.

00:40:42.940 --> 00:40:44.200
I don't know what that means now.

00:40:44.200 --> 00:40:45.400
I don't know you would --

00:40:45.400 --> 00:40:46.790
&gt;&gt; ALBRIGHT: We can talk about that sometime.

00:40:46.790 --> 00:40:47.790
[Laughter.]

00:40:47.790 --> 00:40:48.790
&gt;&gt; KHAR: Yes.

00:40:48.790 --> 00:40:52.970
So allow me to say this with full confidence,
that the military in Pakistan has as much

00:40:52.970 --> 00:40:59.859
a role and plays as much a role in the decision-making
process as the Pentagon -- I would think maybe

00:40:59.859 --> 00:41:01.830
a little less.

00:41:01.830 --> 00:41:07.630
And interestingly, in the last few years,
if you look at what this democratic government

00:41:07.630 --> 00:41:11.570
was able to do with the military -- they're
part of the executive, so we must not be able

00:41:11.570 --> 00:41:15.090
to look at them -- I think, again, this is
a hangover of the past, and I don't blame

00:41:15.090 --> 00:41:16.090
anyone.

00:41:16.090 --> 00:41:17.090
I don't blame our people.

00:41:17.090 --> 00:41:18.090
I don't blame your people.

00:41:18.090 --> 00:41:23.609
If Pakistan was under dictatorial regime for
30-plus years, for 36 years of our history,

00:41:23.609 --> 00:41:25.830
of course people are going to ask those questions.

00:41:25.830 --> 00:41:30.230
But the military operation, for instance,
that we were able to do in Malakand, in Swat

00:41:30.230 --> 00:41:36.480
area of Pakistan, which was able to achieve
rich dividends, was done under the guidance

00:41:36.480 --> 00:41:38.220
of the political leadership.

00:41:38.220 --> 00:41:42.550
It required for the prime minister at that
time to call in an all-parties conference,

00:41:42.550 --> 00:41:45.420
consult all political leaders, create the
space.

00:41:45.420 --> 00:41:47.690
That is the way it is done in any other country.

00:41:47.690 --> 00:41:51.800
And therefore, we feel that it is important
that everybody takes care of their sphere

00:41:51.800 --> 00:41:55.910
of responsibility -- the United States its
own, we ours.

00:41:55.910 --> 00:42:01.920
And I'm glad you alluded to the question of
respect vis-a-vis the conduct of foreign relations.

00:42:01.920 --> 00:42:06.370
Madam Albright, as -- having served as secretary
of state, I think you're right on spot.

00:42:06.370 --> 00:42:12.560
We must all be seen to be pursuing our own
national agenda and our own national interest.

00:42:12.560 --> 00:42:14.990
And then the convergence comes.

00:42:14.990 --> 00:42:20.720
If we can demonstrate that to the people of
Pakistan, which I'm quite sure is the case,

00:42:20.720 --> 00:42:24.330
we would have won this battle and we would
not be talking about the challenges to the

00:42:24.330 --> 00:42:26.160
relations between U.S. and Pakistan.

00:42:26.160 --> 00:42:32.859
So when I said from the podium that if stability
and peace in Afghanistan is your interest

00:42:32.859 --> 00:42:38.310
and the reason for your presence in Afghanistan,
then I see a complete convergence of our national

00:42:38.310 --> 00:42:43.260
interest, because stability and peace in Afghanistan
is clearly a core national interest for Pakistan

00:42:43.260 --> 00:42:44.580
also.

00:42:44.580 --> 00:42:49.710
Then you talked about developing a mechanism
and clarity on that and if there are any incidents.

00:42:49.710 --> 00:42:54.349
I think what requires for that is greater
confidence, and I think we are building on

00:42:54.349 --> 00:42:58.330
the greater confidence, because there are
many mechanisms that exist at various levels,

00:42:58.330 --> 00:43:04.870
but sometimes -- I'm going to steal your phrase
again -- the unintended consequences of the

00:43:04.870 --> 00:43:07.800
decisions that we make and the decisions that
we do not make.

00:43:07.800 --> 00:43:13.609
For instance, allow me to share with you,
I think it did not have a very good effect

00:43:13.609 --> 00:43:18.450
on the Pakistani side --[inaudible] -- inspire
a lot of confidence and mutual respect when

00:43:18.450 --> 00:43:24.869
we lost 24 soldiers, and it took a while before
we were able to, you know, say, let's move

00:43:24.869 --> 00:43:25.869
on.

00:43:25.869 --> 00:43:28.330
It's just things like that which leave lasting
impressions.

00:43:28.330 --> 00:43:32.440
And I remember at that time I used to also
say, I do not fear for today; I fear for what

00:43:32.440 --> 00:43:33.440
we are doing for tomorrow.

00:43:33.440 --> 00:43:38.760
So I think if we have that -- the other thing
that I would like to talk about is -- because

00:43:38.760 --> 00:43:42.680
you talked about what would it take -- I think
what it would take, certainly in the case

00:43:42.680 --> 00:43:46.330
of Afghanistan -- though I don't like U.S.-Pakistan
relations only to be looked at through the

00:43:46.330 --> 00:43:50.560
Afghan lens, but it is true that today the
biggest task that we have to achieve together

00:43:50.560 --> 00:43:54.640
is to work together within the Afghan context.

00:43:54.640 --> 00:44:00.340
And what will clearly inspire a lot of trust
and confidence is the clarity on what we wish

00:44:00.340 --> 00:44:06.470
to achieve in Afghanistan, because, as I said,
the entry goals in Afghanistan are very different

00:44:06.470 --> 00:44:09.270
than the goals that we seem to be talking
about today.

00:44:09.270 --> 00:44:15.040
I think we need complete clarity on what is
it that we wish to achieve in the next 14,

00:44:15.040 --> 00:44:18.190
18 months, and then work backwards from it.

00:44:18.190 --> 00:44:20.839
I'm quite sure we will be able to achieve
much more that way.

00:44:20.839 --> 00:44:21.839
&gt;&gt; ALBRIGHT: All right.

00:44:21.839 --> 00:44:28.920
Let me turn to the audience and -- can you
please rise and tell me your -- identify yourself?

00:44:28.920 --> 00:44:35.580
&gt;&gt; QUESTIONER: My name is Peter Baumbusch
-- my name is Peter Baumbusch.

00:44:35.580 --> 00:44:36.619
I am a lawyer in Washington.

00:44:36.619 --> 00:44:43.300
I'd like to quickly raise three subjects,
which I think are on most people's minds.

00:44:43.300 --> 00:44:50.180
The Taliban -- there's a suggestion, at least
by reading the newspapers, that official sources

00:44:50.180 --> 00:44:53.820
in Pakistan support the Taliban against U.S.
interest.

00:44:53.820 --> 00:44:55.250
I'm not sure that's true.

00:44:55.250 --> 00:44:56.800
I'd love your comment.

00:44:56.800 --> 00:45:03.460
Second, the Haqqani network -- the same thing;
either it is supported or condoned, its leaders

00:45:03.460 --> 00:45:09.540
live in Pakistan without reprisal or problem,
they are killing U.S. soldiers -- that's the

00:45:09.540 --> 00:45:10.540
perception.

00:45:10.540 --> 00:45:12.609
What is the policy with respect to that?

00:45:12.609 --> 00:45:14.500
Third, drone strikes.

00:45:14.500 --> 00:45:22.840
I understand the concern of the Pakistanis
that American drone strikes are a difficulty

00:45:22.840 --> 00:45:24.180
for Pakistan.

00:45:24.180 --> 00:45:27.740
They take away sovereignty and so forth -- sovereignty.

00:45:27.740 --> 00:45:31.869
But understand, from the United States' perspective,
they have actually been quite --

00:45:31.869 --> 00:45:33.170
&gt;&gt; ALBRIGHT: You've asked three difficult
questions to --

00:45:33.170 --> 00:45:34.170
&gt;&gt; QUESTIONER: -- OK -- very effective.

00:45:34.170 --> 00:45:35.770
And I'd be interested in your comments.

00:45:35.770 --> 00:45:36.770
Thank you very much.

00:45:36.770 --> 00:45:37.770
&gt;&gt; KHAR: OK.

00:45:37.770 --> 00:45:40.260
I think your first two questions are the same,
because you talked about the Taliban and the

00:45:40.260 --> 00:45:44.200
Haqqani Network, and there can be many other
entities, but they're all the same.

00:45:44.200 --> 00:45:50.109
If there are any Afghan nationals who are
living in Pakistan -- we have 3 [million]

00:45:50.109 --> 00:45:53.190
-- 5 million all together -- I'm just trying
to give you a perspective -- we have 5 million

00:45:53.190 --> 00:45:56.250
Afghan nationals who live inside Pakistan.

00:45:56.250 --> 00:46:01.280
So we are asked to be responsible for the
activities of some of them.

00:46:01.280 --> 00:46:05.640
We have no wherewithal to be responsible -- or
we have no capacity to be responsible for

00:46:05.640 --> 00:46:10.630
the activities of 5 million of them, because
there are many, many challenges on this track

00:46:10.630 --> 00:46:12.460
that we already suffer from.

00:46:12.460 --> 00:46:17.680
But allow me to share this with you clearly
and categorically, that Pakistan -- like any

00:46:17.680 --> 00:46:22.750
intelligence agency -- let's be honest with
it -- CIA, any intelligence agency -- to maintain

00:46:22.750 --> 00:46:27.859
contact -- CIA has many contacts with many
of these groups also.

00:46:27.859 --> 00:46:34.790
So to maintain contact versus having control
-- that's not a very thin line.

00:46:34.790 --> 00:46:39.790
But unfortunately, it is always misunderstood
and misperceived.

00:46:39.790 --> 00:46:44.160
If we have any contacts with any entities,
our message to those entities is always -- is,

00:46:44.160 --> 00:46:46.950
be part of the political process.

00:46:46.950 --> 00:46:51.109
And as far as the bigger question of Afghan
presence -- or presence of Afghan nationals

00:46:51.109 --> 00:46:54.609
in Pakistan is concerned, I think we need
to find serious answers to that, because we

00:46:54.609 --> 00:47:01.200
will be very happy to look for border controls,
for biometric system, for ensuring that as

00:47:01.200 --> 00:47:07.869
53,000 people cross the border in and out
every day we can have -- and I'll take just

00:47:07.869 --> 00:47:11.140
one minute to give you one example.

00:47:11.140 --> 00:47:14.290
I have -- I don't have very good memories
of my last visit to the United States -- actually,

00:47:14.290 --> 00:47:19.520
the visit before last -- because as I arrived,
we had a statement -- as I arrived with -- (inaudible)

00:47:19.520 --> 00:47:22.820
-- just two days after I had a very extensive
and good meeting with Secretary Clinton, we

00:47:22.820 --> 00:47:27.820
had a statement by Admiral Mullen that the
Haqqani Network is a veritable arm of the

00:47:27.820 --> 00:47:28.820
United States (sic).

00:47:28.820 --> 00:47:29.850
And do you know what inspired that statement?

00:47:29.850 --> 00:47:30.850
&gt;&gt; QUESTIONER: ISAF.

00:47:30.850 --> 00:47:31.850
&gt;&gt; KHAR: ISAF --

00:47:31.850 --> 00:47:32.850
&gt;&gt; ALBRIGHT: (Off mic) --

00:47:32.850 --> 00:47:33.850
&gt;&gt; KHAR: -- or maybe -- OK.

00:47:33.850 --> 00:47:37.500
That was a -- that was not a Freudian slip.

00:47:37.500 --> 00:47:38.500
(Laughter.)

00:47:38.500 --> 00:47:39.500
OK.

00:47:39.500 --> 00:47:45.260
So what inspired that statement was we were
indeed -- intelligence was shared with us

00:47:45.260 --> 00:47:50.220
that there was a truck, which was carrying
some ammo, which was crossing the border and

00:47:50.220 --> 00:47:52.869
going into -- and targeting Kabul.

00:47:52.869 --> 00:47:57.050
Our intelligence -- that information was shared
with us, and we were asked to apprehend it.

00:47:57.050 --> 00:48:02.730
And this was about a 50-kilometer or so area,
where -- within Pakistan.

00:48:02.730 --> 00:48:07.770
So we were unable to apprehend it -- as I
said, an overestimation of capacity.

00:48:07.770 --> 00:48:14.920
However, my question is that, when it crossed
the Pakistani border and traveled 300 kilometers

00:48:14.920 --> 00:48:19.900
inside of one territory, were the Pakistanis
responsible for that?

00:48:19.900 --> 00:48:22.830
So how does the onus of all the responsibility
come to us?

00:48:22.830 --> 00:48:33.000
So, as I said, I think more trust, more realization
of serious capacity issues, of serious constraints

00:48:33.000 --> 00:48:37.580
and working together will obviously help us
move forward.

00:48:37.580 --> 00:48:42.610
So we hold no grief -- we hold no love for
any of the entities that you mentioned, anyone

00:48:42.610 --> 00:48:48.580
-- any entity, whatever name it carries -- any
entity which uses violence as a means to kill

00:48:48.580 --> 00:48:54.750
Afghans is as much a threat to Pakistan's
security, is a threat to Pakistan's national

00:48:54.750 --> 00:48:59.119
interest, as those which choose to use violence
against our people.

00:48:59.119 --> 00:49:06.619
And believe me, you know, from -- within these
groups, the -- they're very, very -- it gets

00:49:06.619 --> 00:49:11.690
very murky as to where the goals and objectives
of one group ends and the other begins.

00:49:11.690 --> 00:49:19.320
So we have absolutely no aspiration to -- and
we [are not besotted ?] by any one of them.

00:49:19.320 --> 00:49:21.740
The question of drone strikes is an important
one.

00:49:21.740 --> 00:49:25.170
And the U.S. claim on that, of course, is
that they're more effective and accurate than

00:49:25.170 --> 00:49:26.170
anything than else.

00:49:26.170 --> 00:49:31.180
I do not disagree with that maybe they might
be more accurate, they might be more efficient.

00:49:31.180 --> 00:49:41.570
But we have to see whether our goal and objective
in all of this fight and all of this war is

00:49:41.570 --> 00:49:47.140
to win the battle or concentrate on winning
the war.

00:49:47.140 --> 00:49:53.200
Our claim is that we have to win the war,
because we have suffered from the consequences

00:49:53.200 --> 00:49:57.160
of what we did three decades back, and we
are still suffering from it today.

00:49:57.160 --> 00:49:59.810
And this is just a reemergence of a past mistake.

00:49:59.810 --> 00:50:03.010
We don't want the same thing to happen in
the future also.

00:50:03.010 --> 00:50:07.460
So therefore, when drone strikes are used,
the message that is sent to the Pakistani

00:50:07.460 --> 00:50:13.090
people is that of United States unilateralism,
which is not what it should be and which is

00:50:13.090 --> 00:50:16.560
what -- not what it is, which is not the partnership
that we are trying to build.

00:50:16.560 --> 00:50:22.600
And I'm very confident today that as we engage
with the United States on this issue and on

00:50:22.600 --> 00:50:31.440
others we will be able to find solutions,
because I think the missing link was to just

00:50:31.440 --> 00:50:36.810
-- as I said, just -- let's give ourselves
a window of confidence and trust and see what

00:50:36.810 --> 00:50:37.810
we can achieve.

00:50:37.810 --> 00:50:43.280
I am very confident, if we are able to give
ourselves that window, we will be very unhappy

00:50:43.280 --> 00:50:46.180
with the lost opportunity of the last many
months.

00:50:46.180 --> 00:50:50.120
&gt;&gt; ALBRIGHT: Yes, sir.

00:50:50.120 --> 00:50:54.070
&gt;&gt; QUESTIONER: Thank you.

00:50:54.070 --> 00:50:55.349
My name is Mike Haltzel.

00:50:55.349 --> 00:50:57.480
I'm at Johns Hopkins SAIS.

00:50:57.480 --> 00:51:03.180
Madame Minister, we've been talking a lot
about trust and respect and creating environments

00:51:03.180 --> 00:51:06.020
and capacities.

00:51:06.020 --> 00:51:13.570
We all know that a vulgar anti-Islamic trailer
was made in California several -- whenever

00:51:13.570 --> 00:51:18.580
it was, a few months ago -- and aired, and
it's -- we all know the ramifications around

00:51:18.580 --> 00:51:19.940
the globe.

00:51:19.940 --> 00:51:25.260
I understand that your government gave a holiday
today to people, saying that it was a way

00:51:25.260 --> 00:51:27.070
to enhance security.

00:51:27.070 --> 00:51:30.400
Some people think it may have the opposite
effect.

00:51:30.400 --> 00:51:33.890
Let me ask this question.

00:51:33.890 --> 00:51:37.600
You studied in the United States, you lived
in the United States; I daresay I suspect

00:51:37.600 --> 00:51:44.200
you know that the U.S. government is not anti-Islam.

00:51:44.200 --> 00:51:48.570
The president, the secretary of state have
put ads on your television, saying that.

00:51:48.570 --> 00:51:55.250
Wouldn't it be a good idea for your president,
your prime minister, or even you to go on

00:51:55.250 --> 00:52:00.440
television -- you have a lot more credibility
than we do with your own people -- and say,

00:52:00.440 --> 00:52:05.551
the United States is an important country
with which we differ on many issues; they

00:52:05.551 --> 00:52:10.640
have a different culture; they have a First
Amendment; it may be difficult for us to understand,

00:52:10.640 --> 00:52:13.840
but we can assure you they are not anti-Muslim?

00:52:13.840 --> 00:52:16.950
Would that not help?

00:52:16.950 --> 00:52:18.040
Is that possible?

00:52:18.040 --> 00:52:22.000
Wouldn't that create the kind of mutual trust
and respect that you're talking about?

00:52:22.000 --> 00:52:27.770
&gt;&gt; KHAR: I think, to be quite honest, there
are two different things, unfortunately, which

00:52:27.770 --> 00:52:32.970
are getting mixed up in this particular -- you
know, in this particular problem.

00:52:32.970 --> 00:52:37.510
There is one -- let's admit it -- and I think
that this is where we have to have a deeper

00:52:37.510 --> 00:52:39.480
understanding of each other's cultures, each
other's religions.

00:52:39.480 --> 00:52:41.980
First of all, Islam is a religion of peace.

00:52:41.980 --> 00:52:47.440
So this jihadi mindset, which is imposed on
Islam -- let me reject it completely.

00:52:47.440 --> 00:52:53.030
Allow me to say also that in Islam, desecration
of any religion -- of any religious figure,

00:52:53.030 --> 00:52:58.020
even those of Christianity, even that of Jesus,
and certainly that of Prophet Muhammad is

00:52:58.020 --> 00:53:00.490
considered to be wrong.

00:53:00.490 --> 00:53:04.420
So I think we need to develop more understanding
in understanding each other's sensitivities.

00:53:04.420 --> 00:53:09.610
I am, of course, happy to -- and I know that
many of my other colleagues in Pakistan government

00:53:09.610 --> 00:53:14.450
have already done so -- I was just watching,
you know, Information Minister Kaira, who

00:53:14.450 --> 00:53:20.000
was saying exactly what you asked us to say,
and I'm happy to say that President -- that

00:53:20.000 --> 00:53:27.930
President Obama's words, timely words, Secretary
Clinton's timely words on this are very, very

00:53:27.930 --> 00:53:28.930
appreciated.

00:53:28.930 --> 00:53:32.270
And they've said that this is disgusting and
that the U.S. government by no means supports

00:53:32.270 --> 00:53:33.270
anything like this.

00:53:33.270 --> 00:53:38.940
And it is for that reason that in Pakistan
you did not see a reaction in the first week.

00:53:38.940 --> 00:53:45.840
But there will always be forces who will use
these opportunities to get their own designs.

00:53:45.840 --> 00:53:48.590
And in -- again, I think you need to trust
us with that.

00:53:48.590 --> 00:53:55.890
In declaring it a holiday, we think we were
able to manage it better than if it had not.

00:53:55.890 --> 00:54:00.450
If it had not been declared a holiday, the
schoolchildren, the office-goers would have

00:54:00.450 --> 00:54:02.070
all been at risk.

00:54:02.070 --> 00:54:04.670
People would have had more of an opportunity
to congregate.

00:54:04.670 --> 00:54:09.960
The type of security measures that the government
was able to take by putting in strong, you

00:54:09.960 --> 00:54:14.760
know -- all sorts of things in front of -- diplomatic
missions, et cetera, we think we have been

00:54:14.760 --> 00:54:19.440
able to protect our -- and that is our responsibility
to do so.

00:54:19.440 --> 00:54:25.750
So, again, let us -- you know, allow us the
privilege of knowing how to deal with a situation

00:54:25.750 --> 00:54:27.160
as it arises.

00:54:27.160 --> 00:54:33.530
But I think what is important is that on -- we
don't misinterpret this to be entirely anti-Americanism.

00:54:33.530 --> 00:54:36.750
This is against one person.

00:54:36.750 --> 00:54:39.010
This is against what he did.

00:54:39.010 --> 00:54:43.670
And I think -- I have no qualms in saying
this, exactly what you said, that the United

00:54:43.670 --> 00:54:46.599
States government's response on this has been
very good.

00:54:46.599 --> 00:54:51.010
The United States government, the president,
Secretary Clinton's -- Senator Kerry, as he

00:54:51.010 --> 00:54:54.840
met me yesterday, gave a very strong statement
about this, that all of you, the government

00:54:54.840 --> 00:55:01.650
feels this is disgusting, that it is not something
that you in any way -- so there's some deeper

00:55:01.650 --> 00:55:04.430
issues, but what was required immediately
management.

00:55:04.430 --> 00:55:10.010
And believe me, we are trying to manage it.

00:55:10.010 --> 00:55:11.870
&gt;&gt; QUESTIONER: Thank you.

00:55:11.870 --> 00:55:17.740
Barbara Slavin from the Atlantic Council and
from Al-Monitor.com.

00:55:17.740 --> 00:55:20.359
Thank you very much for this opportunity.

00:55:20.359 --> 00:55:25.619
You talked about the 24 soldiers that were
killed, but you didn't mention Osama bin Laden.

00:55:25.619 --> 00:55:29.940
And many in this country still want to see
a full investigation, to understand how it

00:55:29.940 --> 00:55:35.839
was that he lived all that time in Abbottabad
without your government, apparently, knowing

00:55:35.839 --> 00:55:38.220
about his presence.

00:55:38.220 --> 00:55:42.010
In terms of restoring trust, what are you
doing on this front?

00:55:42.010 --> 00:55:46.640
And also, yes, you say that these demonstrations
are against an individual who made a film

00:55:46.640 --> 00:55:53.000
and not anti-American, but what can you do
to help Americans feel that they can participate

00:55:53.000 --> 00:55:58.490
and help Pakistan when there is a real security
threat to many of them if they try to go out

00:55:58.490 --> 00:55:59.840
in Pakistani society?

00:55:59.840 --> 00:56:01.070
Thank you.

00:56:01.070 --> 00:56:02.320
&gt;&gt; KHAR: OK.

00:56:02.320 --> 00:56:06.400
First of all, on Osama bin Laden, as you know,
we have had an independent commission, which

00:56:06.400 --> 00:56:08.950
was set up, which is investigating this matter.

00:56:08.950 --> 00:56:13.670
And it is surprising, because even though
today's in the age of -- information age and

00:56:13.670 --> 00:56:19.220
information travels very quickly, but what
makes headlines here typically -- what makes

00:56:19.220 --> 00:56:22.849
headlines in Pakistan typically do not make
headlines -- (inaudible).

00:56:22.849 --> 00:56:28.369
For instance, when Osama bin Laden -- it was
found that he was living in that compound,

00:56:28.369 --> 00:56:33.220
the reaction from Pakistani society was even
more hostile than maybe yours.

00:56:33.220 --> 00:56:36.450
They were even more shocked at it than you.

00:56:36.450 --> 00:56:41.200
However, from your intelligence reports -- because
you know a lot of intelligence was gathered

00:56:41.200 --> 00:56:46.170
from here -- it is now clear that Pakistani
state or any organ of the state had no knowledge

00:56:46.170 --> 00:56:47.170
of it.

00:56:47.170 --> 00:56:50.720
However, we are awaiting the report of the
independent commission.

00:56:50.720 --> 00:56:54.800
And why we have not said or done anything
on it is because I think we need to give that

00:56:54.800 --> 00:56:56.300
process time.

00:56:56.300 --> 00:56:58.630
That report is going to be out in October.

00:56:58.630 --> 00:57:02.270
And once that report is out, then we will
see, obviously, what needs to be done about

00:57:02.270 --> 00:57:03.270
it.

00:57:03.270 --> 00:57:05.430
And we are -- we have not -- why don't we
continue to talk about it?

00:57:05.430 --> 00:57:10.080
Because I think we need to give that independent
commission the time to be able to deal with

00:57:10.080 --> 00:57:11.080
it.

00:57:11.080 --> 00:57:15.160
&gt;&gt; ALBRIGHT: I have to ask here -- the treatment
of the doctor, I think, is something --

00:57:15.160 --> 00:57:16.160
&gt;&gt; KHAR: Yeah, I know.

00:57:16.160 --> 00:57:18.339
&gt;&gt; ALBRIGHT: -- that is of great concern to
many Americans.

00:57:18.339 --> 00:57:19.359
&gt;&gt; KHAR: Yes.

00:57:19.359 --> 00:57:20.359
OK.

00:57:20.359 --> 00:57:27.640
First of all, again, I think there's a serious
communication gap and a difference of how

00:57:27.640 --> 00:57:30.500
we view one people or the other.

00:57:30.500 --> 00:57:33.460
Osama bin Laden was no hero for Pakistan.

00:57:33.460 --> 00:57:37.060
Osama bin Laden -- first let me talk about
-- because I think it is generally believed

00:57:37.060 --> 00:57:40.829
that we don't want to talk about Osama bin
Laden because we had -- we almost were harboring

00:57:40.829 --> 00:57:41.829
him.

00:57:41.829 --> 00:57:45.070
And we -- he was an enemy to us.

00:57:45.070 --> 00:57:50.349
He killed -- because of his actions and because
of his organizations, more Pakistanis lost

00:57:50.349 --> 00:57:53.810
their lives than maybe 20 other single organization.

00:57:53.810 --> 00:57:59.950
We -- as I said before, many al-Qaida operatives
we have been able to go against very, very

00:57:59.950 --> 00:58:00.970
effectively.

00:58:00.970 --> 00:58:04.160
So he was no hero to Pakistan.

00:58:04.160 --> 00:58:08.810
In the same way, we feel Dr. Shakil Afridi
should be no hero to the Americans.

00:58:08.810 --> 00:58:10.280
And let me build a case for that.

00:58:10.280 --> 00:58:14.349
First of all, it is now well known -- and
I'm sure your own information will corroborate

00:58:14.349 --> 00:58:19.500
that -- that Dr. Shakil Afridi did not know
the herculean task that he was trying to do.

00:58:19.500 --> 00:58:22.180
He did know that he was going after Osama
bin Laden.

00:58:22.180 --> 00:58:26.460
He was a man who was up for hire by anybody
who was willing to pay him, and that included

00:58:26.460 --> 00:58:35.040
Islamic organizations, terrorist organizations,
which were using him to move and work against

00:58:35.040 --> 00:58:37.120
your and our interests.

00:58:37.120 --> 00:58:39.050
So he was no hero.

00:58:39.050 --> 00:58:46.540
What we have to do right now is to await the
legal process to take its full course.

00:58:46.540 --> 00:58:50.400
And as people who believe in the rule of law,
I think we should allow the process -- he

00:58:50.400 --> 00:58:58.579
does have many, you know, ways where he can
go back and take those decisions back -- you

00:58:58.579 --> 00:59:02.020
know, appeal processes -- he has many appeal
processes.

00:59:02.020 --> 00:59:09.030
Lastly, I am very, very concerned about what
this one man could do to the future of polio

00:59:09.030 --> 00:59:11.109
and children in Pakistan.

00:59:11.109 --> 00:59:17.450
His activity has ensured that today caregivers
and people who want to work for polio are

00:59:17.450 --> 00:59:19.150
not welcomed.

00:59:19.150 --> 00:59:22.550
This was a huge battle of 20 years that we
had to fight in Pakistan.

00:59:22.550 --> 00:59:29.490
And because of this one single man's activity,
polio today is an emerging concern in Pakistan.

00:59:29.490 --> 00:59:34.869
So what I would very much -- and because I
know we're here -- we have people who make

00:59:34.869 --> 00:59:39.540
the opinion and therefore the policy, I think
opinion-makers today have more importance

00:59:39.540 --> 00:59:40.540
than people like us.

00:59:40.540 --> 00:59:44.430
We just have to follow the opinion you create
and somehow deal with it.

00:59:44.430 --> 00:59:51.510
So I would urge you to allow -- not allow
this one single man to be the demonstration

00:59:51.510 --> 00:59:56.820
of Pakistan-U.S. relations and not to put
-- not to put us hostage to the future of

00:59:56.820 --> 00:59:57.820
this.

00:59:57.820 --> 01:00:00.810
And I can assure you that he has appeal process
that is available to him.

01:00:00.810 --> 01:00:03.180
His lawyers are going through that appeal
process.

01:00:03.180 --> 01:00:04.890
Let it run its course.

01:00:04.890 --> 01:00:07.010
But let's take this man for what he was.

01:00:07.010 --> 01:00:13.130
Let's not valorize a man who may be seen over
here to be pursuing something that he clearly

01:00:13.130 --> 01:00:14.130
wasn't.

01:00:14.130 --> 01:00:17.320
&gt;&gt; ALBRIGHT: We'll take one more question
in the back there.

01:00:17.320 --> 01:00:18.520
&gt;&gt; QUESTIONER: Thank you very much.

01:00:18.520 --> 01:00:21.829
(Name inaudible) -- I work for Voice of America,
Pashto -- (inaudible) -- border region.

01:00:21.829 --> 01:00:23.000
It's called Deewa Radio.

01:00:23.000 --> 01:00:24.350
Thank you very much for your comments.

01:00:24.350 --> 01:00:31.400
My question is -- you said that Pakistan-Afghanistan
long border poses also challenges in the future.

01:00:31.400 --> 01:00:36.960
So taking into the consideration the Pakistan
role in the future of Afghanistan, especially

01:00:36.960 --> 01:00:44.680
talking to Taliban, which also brings into
the fold ISI -- so is it jeopardizing the

01:00:44.680 --> 01:00:51.190
Pakistan efforts to play its role, number
one; and second, why do you think that we

01:00:51.190 --> 01:00:55.720
don't see any progress on talks with Taliban?

01:00:55.720 --> 01:01:00.630
So does it mean they have -- the United States
and Pakistan has differences over the -- how

01:01:00.630 --> 01:01:03.160
to reach out to Taliban?

01:01:03.160 --> 01:01:04.320
Is that the main reason?

01:01:04.320 --> 01:01:06.470
Or what is holding them back?

01:01:06.470 --> 01:01:07.470
Thank you very much.

01:01:07.470 --> 01:01:08.470
&gt;&gt; KHAR: OK.

01:01:08.470 --> 01:01:12.660
Again, something that I referred to earlier,
where I said -- OK, let me talk about this

01:01:12.660 --> 01:01:13.730
from the beginning.

01:01:13.730 --> 01:01:18.550
Play its role -- I find that this term sometimes
can be misinterpreted, so I don't like to

01:01:18.550 --> 01:01:21.020
use this term, that -- what role will Pakistan
play.

01:01:21.020 --> 01:01:27.060
I think the role that Pakistan can play is
that of a facilitator, the facilitator of

01:01:27.060 --> 01:01:31.770
whatever the Afghan people decide to be the
course of action to take.

01:01:31.770 --> 01:01:38.270
And allow me to say that we feel rather strongly
that these peace deals cannot be brokered

01:01:38.270 --> 01:01:39.910
anywhere else but in Kabul.

01:01:39.910 --> 01:01:43.869
The terms and conditions of these peace deals
also have to be led by Kabul.

01:01:43.869 --> 01:01:49.540
So there is maybe a need for more intensive
intra-Afghan dialogue, within their society,

01:01:49.540 --> 01:01:55.170
because typically we've seen that efforts
to impose terms and conditions will -- on

01:01:55.170 --> 01:01:57.420
the Afghan people have not served our purposes.

01:01:57.420 --> 01:02:02.020
So the role that Pakistan can play is only
one role, that of a facilitator.

01:02:02.020 --> 01:02:05.260
We are willing to go out of our way to play
the role of a facilitator.

01:02:05.260 --> 01:02:09.340
As far as the future of Afghan-Pakistan relations
are concerned, we only wish -- as I said,

01:02:09.340 --> 01:02:15.690
we only see strategic depth in having a relation
with Afghanistan -- with an independent, sovereign

01:02:15.690 --> 01:02:18.220
(Afghan ?), which are based on trust.

01:02:18.220 --> 01:02:24.589
And we are -- our only interest is to have
bilateral relations with Afghanistan as a

01:02:24.589 --> 01:02:26.030
sovereign equal.

01:02:26.030 --> 01:02:30.031
Because of the past, there will be many misperceptions
on what Pakistan wants to do.

01:02:30.031 --> 01:02:34.510
But I can assure you that as we will go through
the next few months and next two years -- and

01:02:34.510 --> 01:02:38.280
as you look through the last two years, three
years, ever since this government came in,

01:02:38.280 --> 01:02:44.079
I think it should inspire a lot of confidence
in how Pakistan expects to build its relations

01:02:44.079 --> 01:02:45.079
with Afghanistan.

01:02:45.079 --> 01:02:47.021
&gt;&gt; ALBRIGHT: Madam Minister, thank you very,
very much.

01:02:47.021 --> 01:02:48.021
(Applause.)

01:02:48.021 --> 01:02:49.021
Your country is lucky to have you as its foreign
minister.

01:02:49.021 --> 01:02:50.021
&gt;&gt; KHAR: Thank you.

01:02:50.021 --> 01:02:51.021
Thank you very much.

01:02:51.021 --> 01:02:52.021
&gt;&gt; ALBRIGHT: Thank you.

01:02:52.021 --> 01:02:53.021
MR.

01:02:53.021 --> 01:02:54.021
: Thank you very much.

01:02:54.021 --> 01:02:55.021
&gt;&gt; ALBRIGHT: Thank you all very much.

01:02:55.021 --> 01:02:56.021
